  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIt will be a mild start to the day with temperatures in the 60s\nfor most areas as warm air advection continues ahead of an\napproaching cold front. Partial sunshine for most areas will\nallow for temperatures to warm quickly after sunrise prior to\nthe arrival of the front and rain showers. Most areas outside of\nthe Adirondacks will rise into the 70s except lower 80s are \nlikely for portions of the mid-Hudson Valley and Litchfield \nCounty, CT. These values will fall short of current daily \nrecord highs. Rain showers associated with a cold front will \ntrack northwest to southeast during the day reaching areas north\nand west of Albany this morning and areas south and east this\nafternoon. Wet bulb effects shortly after the onset of rain \nwill cause temperatures to fall back through the 70s into the \n60s. There may be some weak instability present across portions \nof the mid- Hudson Valley into Litchfield County, CT for a \nrumble of thunder to occur if any taller shower can develop. \nRainfall, overall, will be rather light with most areas picking \nup 0.15 inches or less, though some locally higher amounts up to\n0.50 inches are possible across portions of the Adirondacks as \nwell as the mid- Hudson Valley into Litchfield County, CT should\na thunderstorm develop. Still, no hydrological impacts are \nexpected. Rain showers fully exit the region by this evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region behind the cold front\ntonight and tracks across the region this weekend. Saturday will\nbe a very nice day with plenty of sun and near seasonable\ntemperatures. Similar temperatures are expected on Sunday but\nthere will be an increase in high clouds during the day as\nseparate northern and southern stream systems approach. Sunday\nlooks to remain dry. The best rain chances will be for areas\nnorth and west of Albany Sunday night into Monday as a strong \ncold front approaches from the Great Lakes region. This front \ncrosses the region on Monday bringing breezy conditions and a \nbrief push of much cooler air as 850 hPa temperatures fall to \nbelow 0C. High temperatures in the 50s and 60s Monday will trend\ndown to the upper 40s to upper 50s on Tuesday. Temperatures \nlook to fall back into the 30s for most areas Tuesday night \nwhich would increase the potential for frost formation, \nincluding within the Hudson Valley into Litchfield County, CT \nwhere the growing season continues. Temperatures rebound back to\nnear normal values for the mid to late week.",
